\description{
Accumulates the score from one end, one score value at a time, and finds
the cut that maximizes the value of the decision under volume, budget,
daily capacity and event-rate constraints: how many customers to target,
how many alerts to raise, or how many applicants to approve.
}
\section{Side}{


\code{side = "event"} selects from the event-rich end of the score (targeting
under propensity, alerting under fraud, collections under credit);
\code{side = "safe"} accepts from the safe end (approval under credit). The
default follows the objective and the direction: propensity selects from
the event-rich end (\code{"event"}); risk with \code{higher_is_riskier} (fraud)
alerts from the event-rich end (\code{"event"}); risk with \code{higher_is_safer}
(credit) approves from the safe end (\code{"safe"}).

The selected rows are \code{score >= cut} when the selection starts at the high
scores and \code{score < cut} when it starts at the low ones, the convention of
\code{\link[=scr_cutoff]{scr_cutoff()}}. Every cut sits between two adjacent distinct scores (or on
a bucket edge when the scores were pooled into \code{max_cells} cells), as in
\code{\link[=scr_bands]{scr_bands()}}; the last row of the curve selects every row (\code{cut} is
\code{-Inf} or \code{Inf}).
}

\section{Curve}{


One row per candidate cut, in increasing depth: \code{cut}, \code{depth} (share of
the volume selected), \code{n_sel}, \code{events_sel}, \code{rate_sel} with its Jeffreys
interval \code{rate_lo}, \code{rate_hi} (on the Kish effective size under weights),
\code{capture} (share of all events selected), \code{lift} (\code{rate_sel} over the
overall rate), \code{marginal_rate} (the event rate of the score value just
added, smoothed by pool adjacent violators toward the event-rich end of
the score), \code{cost} (\code{cost_select * n_sel}), \code{value} and \code{feasible}. With
a date, \code{day_q} (the \code{day_quantile} quantile of the selected volume per
day) and \code{pct_days_over} (share of days above \code{max_per_day}).

The economics:
\itemize{
\item side \code{"event"}: \code{value = gain_event * events_sel - cost_select * n_sel}; with a \code{value} column and no \code{gain_event}, the sum of the
value of the selected events replaces \code{gain_event * events_sel}.
\item side \code{"safe"}: \code{value = revenue_good * nonevents_sel - loss_bad * events_sel - cost_select * n_sel}, the cumulative profit of
\code{\link[=scr_strategy]{scr_strategy()}} when \code{cost_select = 0} (a missing one of
\code{revenue_good} and \code{loss_bad} counts as 0).
}
Without economics (\code{gain_event}, a \code{value} column, \code{revenue_good} or
\code{loss_bad}), \code{value} is \code{NA}. Non-events are rows with a known outcome
that are not events; rows with a missing outcome count in the volume and
the cost only.

The curve is thinned to about \code{n_points} rows evenly spread in depth; the
rows of the optimum, the last row meeting each constraint, the deepest
row and the rows nearest 1\%, 5\%, 10\%, 20\% and 50\% are always kept. The
optimum and the constraints are evaluated on every cell boundary.
}

\section{Constraints and optimum}{


\code{max_n} (\code{n_sel <= max_n}), \code{max_share} (\code{depth <= max_share}), \code{budget}
(\code{cost_select * n_sel <= budget}), \code{max_per_day} (\code{day_q <= max_per_day}), \code{min_rate} (\code{rate_sel >= min_rate}, side \code{"event"}) and
\code{max_rate} (\code{rate_sel <= max_rate}, the event rate among the accepted,
side \code{"safe"}). A row is feasible when it meets every constraint given.

The optimum is the feasible row with the highest value (the smallest
depth on a tie); without economics, the deepest feasible row. A
constraint is \code{binding} when dropping it alone, the others kept,
improves the optimum: a higher value, or a greater depth when there are
no economics. A constraint that is slack at the optimum is therefore
never named, and a constraint that stops the curve at the row that is
the best anyway is not binding either. Constraints that stop the optimum
at the same row bind jointly (none improves it alone) and are named
together. When no constraint binds, \code{binding} is \code{"value"} with
economics (no row is worth more than the optimum) and \code{"end of the curve"} without (every row is selected).
\code{shadow_price} is the marginal value of the next score value beyond the
optimum, per additional selected case: \code{gain_event * marginal_rate - cost_select} on side \code{"event"} (with a \code{value} column, the smoothed event
value per case of that score value), \code{revenue_good * (1 - marginal_rate) - loss_bad * marginal_rate - cost_select} on side
\code{"safe"}. It is what one more selected case is worth when a constraint
binds; divide it by \code{cost_select} for the value of one more unit of
budget. When no row is feasible, the optimum is \code{NA} and a warning names
the constraints that the first row already breaks.
}

\section{Daily capacity}{


A day is a distinct value of the date column: with a monthly date, read
the capacity per month. For every candidate cut, the selected volume of
each day is a cumulative sum over a table of counts per day and score
value; \code{day_q} is its quantile across the days (type 7 of
\code{\link[stats:quantile]{stats::quantile()}}). The quantile grows with the depth, so the deepest
cut within \code{max_per_day} is found by bisection. Rows with a missing date
count in the curve but not in the daily volumes. A scorecard uses the
dates of its scored sample when they exist. A date-time column counts
every distinct time as a day: convert it with \code{\link[=as.Date]{as.Date()}} first.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
x <- rnorm(5000)
d <- data.frame(score = round(500 + 50 * x),
                y = rbinom(5000, 1, plogis(-1.5 + 1.2 * x)),
                day = as.Date("2026-01-01") + sample(0:29, 5000, TRUE))
# targeting under propensity: a gain per responder, a cost per contact, a budget
op <- scr_operating(d, objective = "propensity", gain_event = 40, cost_select = 6,
                    budget = 6000)
op
op$optimum[, c("cut", "depth", "n_sel", "rate_sel", "value", "binding", "shadow_price")]

# alerting under a daily capacity: at most 40 alerts on nine days in ten
scr_operating(d, objective = "propensity", max_per_day = 40, date = "day")$optimum
}
